  "sections": [
    {
      "selector": "section",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1510,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 476,
      "heading": "Windows leaves that space empty. We didn’t.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "Why the top edge Windows leaves that space empty. We didn’t. The strip above your desktop is bigger than a menu bar and does exactly as much. Crest started as a two-hour experiment to see if that space could hold something useful without turning into another sidebar, another floating window, another thing competing for room. It didn’t need to. It just needed to know when to show up.",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "#features",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1987,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 1046,
      "heading": "See what’s happening without losing focus.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "Panels See what’s happening without losing focus. Seven panels, each a page of the same card. Arrows or a scroll move between them and it morphs to fit. Keep the ones you use, drag them into the order you want. Now playing A track starts. Skip it without touching the taskbar. Quick launcher You need an app right now. Type three letters, it's open. File shelf A file needs to go somewhere else first. Drag it up, drop it later. Notifications Something lands in the Action Center. It surfaces here first, then files itself away. System monitor The fan kicks in. Check what's actually using the CPU be",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "section",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3033,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 572,
      "heading": "Built for the little things.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "The small stuff Built for the little things. Not a redesign of how you use your PC: the small stuff that happens forty times a day. None of it deserves its own window. All of it fits in a strip you already know how to reach. A timer for the next 25 minutes Set it, forget the app exists, glance at the pill when you need to. A glance at what's eating your CPU Before you blame the Wi-Fi, check whether it's actually your machine. The thing you copied three clicks ago Not the one still on your clipboard. The one before that.",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "#settings",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3605,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 932,
      "heading": "Yours to arrange, no config file.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [
        "Panels & colour",
        "Where it lives",
        "What it tells you",
        "Set once"
      ],
      "text": "Settings Yours to arrange, no config file. Crest works the moment it installs, so none of this is setup: it is what you change once you have lived with it for a week. Every change lands immediately, in every window at once. Panels & colour Where it lives What it tells you Set once Choose your panels Switch off the ones you don't use and drag the rest into the order you want. Media Launcher Shelf Calendar +3 Five themes Crest, Glacier, Ember, Daylight and Mono. One repaints every window at once. Accent colour Eight to pick from, or type your own hex. Everything active is drawn in it. Background",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "#how",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 4537,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 1396,
      "heading": "No shortcut to learn.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "How it works No shortcut to learn. The whole interface is one motion you already make a hundred times a day: moving the cursor to the top of the screen. Crest is a cursor-driven overlay, not another window to manage. 01 Install and forget One installer, no setup screen. Crest registers itself to start with Windows and then gets out of the way: there is nothing to configure before it is useful. A thin mark on the top edge shows you where to point until you no longer need it. 02 Nudge the top of the screen A thin strip at the very top edge is the trigger. Touch it and the pill peeks; keep the cu",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "#stack",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 5933,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 905,
      "heading": "Built like a system tool, not a wrapper.",
      "backgroundColor": "#050508",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "Under the hood Built like a system tool, not a wrapper. Native, not a web app in a box The music, the launcher index, the clipboard listener, the drag-out shelf and the load meters are all real Windows APIs called from Rust. The CPU and GPU figures come from the same performance counters Task Manager reads, so they agree with it rather than approximating it. Idle costs nothing Nothing renders while the notch is away, and the watchers that have to keep listening (what is playing, what has just arrived, what your machine is doing) settle to a check every two seconds. The meters only speed up whi",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
